In Java, the `==` operator checks if two references point to the same object in memory. It is not a reliable way to compare strings, as it only checks if the references are equal, not the actual string values.

To compare strings in Java, you should use the `equals()` method. It checks if the content of two strings is equal, regardless of the object references.

Here's an example:

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = "hello";

// Using == operator to compare strings
if (str1 == str2) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are the same object");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are different objects");
}

// Using equals() method to compare strings
if (str1.equals(str2)) {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 have the same content");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 have different content");
}
```

In this example, `str1` and `str2` both contain the same string value, but they are two separate objects in memory. Using `==` will result in `false`, while using `equals()` will result in `true`.

As a general rule, use `equals()` to compare strings, and avoid using `==`. The only exception is when you are comparing string literals, which are guaranteed to refer to the same object in memory. For example:

```java
String str1 = "hello";
String str2 = "hello";

if (str1 == str2) { // This will always be true, since both str1 and str2 refer to the same object in memory
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are the same object");
} else {
    System.out.println("str1 and str2 are different objects");
}
``` 

In this case, you can use `==` safely, as both `str1` and `str2` refer to the same object in memory. However, this is a rare occurrence, and most of the time you should use `equals()`.

